From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] target/arm/ptw: log hardware access flag and dirty state
 updates

With FEAT_HAFDBS, the walker updates descriptors itself: it sets AF on
access and, for DBM, clears AP[2] on the first write. arm_casq_ptw() makes
that update with a cmpxchg through the host pointer the walk found. The
store probe that would mark the page dirty (TLB_NOTDIRTY handling in
probe_access_full_mmu) runs only while the walk doesn't yet know the page
is writable; once it does (the usual case, ptw->out_rw), the update reaches
RAM without a dirty bit. The other path, for a descriptor without a host
pointer (address_space_ldq/stq under the BQL), is already logged by
address_space_stq_*(); only the host pointer path is not.

For live migration this loses guest state. A page-table page sent before
the update is not sent again, so the destination keeps the old descriptor:
for DBM that means the page reads as clean (read-only) there, although the
guest wrote it. Linux uses that bit as the PTE's dirty state, so the
destination kernel can reclaim a modified file page without writing it
back. Lost AF updates are milder (page ageing). Linux arm64 enables HA by
default, and HD where the CPU has it (-cpu max does).

Found through an in-memory snapshot that copies back only dirty pages:
on Ubuntu 24.04 arm64, 7 page-table pages per restore differed by bit 10
(AF) from the snapshot.

Probe for the store in that case too. Only hardware AF/DBM updates pay for
it: one probe, normally a TLB hit, per descriptor update, not per walk.
---
 target/arm/ptw.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/target/arm/ptw.c b/target/arm/ptw.c
index a29de0385f4e9990457d4b8009e8e8ec57888939..9afc1aa0e7c7971cc6a670af8dde781bf8a77492 100644
--- a/target/arm/ptw.c
+++ b/target/arm/ptw.c
@@ -962,6 +962,11 @@ static uint64_t arm_casq_ptw(CPUARMState *env, uint64_t old_val,
     /*
      * Raising a stage2 Protection fault for an atomic update to a read-only
      * page is delayed until it is certain that there is a change to make.
+     *
+     * The store probe also marks the page dirty (TLB_NOTDIRTY handling in
+     * probe_access_full_mmu): the update below writes through the host
+     * pointer, which dirty-memory clients (migration) would otherwise never
+     * see. So probe even when the walk already knows the page is writable.
      */
     if (unlikely(!ptw->out_rw)) {
         int flags;
@@ -990,6 +995,10 @@ static uint64_t arm_casq_ptw(CPUARMState *env, uint64_t old_val,
 
         /* In case CAS mismatches and we loop, remember writability. */
         ptw->out_rw = true;
+    } else {
+        probe_access_full_mmu(env, ptw->out_virt, 0, MMU_DATA_STORE,
+                              arm_to_core_mmu_idx(ptw->in_ptw_idx),
+                              NULL, NULL);
     }
 
     if (ptw->out_be) {
